== Children Getting Parents’ Permission

=== §. 11. #emph[Of childrens obedience.]

The obedience of children doth most prove the authority of parents, and is the surest evidence of the honour a child giveth to his parent: therefore is it #footnote[#emph[Eph.] 6. 1. #emph[Col.] 3. 20.] by name in the text expressed, and all other duties are comprised under it. Reverence without obedience is a mere mockage, nothing at all acceptable. Of the two, a child were better fail in the former: instance the #footnote[#emph[Matt.] 21. 31.] parable of the two sons. Reverence in comparison of obedience is but a complemental honour. Obedience is a true real honour; the surest trial of a dutiful child. Obedience is a duty so proper to children, as the Apostle applieth it to them as a proper attribute, saying, #footnote[1 #emph[Pet.] 1. 14.] #emph[as obedient children fashion not,] etc. The example of Christ is herein set before us as a pattern: #footnote[#emph[Luk.] 2. 51.] #emph[he was subject to his parents. Solomon] counteth the neglect thereof a #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 23. 22.] #emph[despising] of a parent.

Contrary is disobedience and rebellion: the greatest impeachment #footnote[Disobedience most contrary to parents authority.] of parents authority that can be. For to what end is authority over those who resist it, and rebel against it? The #footnote[2 #emph[Tim.] 3. 2.] Apostle reckoneth disobedient children among the lewdest persons that be: and setteth forth their disobedience by #footnote[#emph[Tit.] 1. 6.] a metaphor taken from untamed, head-strong beasts, that will not be brought under the yoke: the word therefore is not unfitly translated #emph[unruly:] and it is somewhat answerable to an #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 13. 13. #emph[ex non & profuit. Hier. absque iugo. quasi]] Hebrew phrase given to disobedient children, viz. #emph[sons of Belial,] which is according to the notation as much as #emph[sons without profit;] or, as some will have it, #emph[sons without yoke,] that is, such children, as refusing to be in subjection unto parents, are no way profitable, but work much mischief, and cause great grief. The #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 21. 18, #emph[etc.]] punishment which by Gods law was appointed to disobedient and rebellious children, was a public shameful death.

=== §. 12. #emph[Of childrens forbearing to do things without consent of parents.]

That children may the better know their duty in this respect, #footnote[The parts of a childs obedience.] I will distinctly set forth, both the #emph[parts,] and also the #emph[extent] of a childs obedience:

- 1\. Wherein it consisteth.
- 2\. How far it extendeth.

The general #emph[parts] wherein it consisteth are two:

1. A #emph[forbearance] from doing things without consent of parents. #footnote[Of the several ways whereby consent may be given, see Treat. 3.]

2. A #emph[performance] of such things as parents will have done.

The former of these is a duty whereunto children are most bound while they are under their parents government. For that #footnote[§. 19. #emph[Num.] 30. 17.] time the consent of parents is not only meet, but necessary: and that for these reasons.

1. Children are as the goods of their parents, wholly in their power, to be ordered and disposed by them. On this ground Satan having all that #emph[Job] had put into his hand, took liberty over #footnote[#emph[Job] 1. 12, 19.] his children as well as over his goods and chattel.

2. Children while they be under government, (even the eldest that are heirs) differ nothing from servants. #footnote[#emph[Galat.] 4. 1.]

3. By Gods law given to the Jews, parents had power to sell #footnote[#emph[Exod.] 21. 7.] their children.

4. Parents had power to disannul such things as children had done. Instance the case of a vow made to God, which was one of #footnote[#emph[Num.] 30. 4.] the most inviolable things that one could do.

Contrary is the opinion and practise of many, who hold parents consent at the most but a matter of conveniency: that it is good, if children will, to have their parents consent: if they have it not, the matter is not great: their contracts or other things which they do, are as firm, and good, without, as with their consents. If this were so, wherein is the authority of a parent more then of a wise experienced friend? It is meet, and good to have such an ones consent.

But that the power of parents, and duty of children in this point, may the better be seen, I will exemplify it in five particular cases. 1. Entering into a calling. 2. Making marriage. 3. Disposing of goods. 4. Ordering apparel. 5. Making vows.

=== §. 13. #emph[Of consent of parents for childrens entering into a calling.]

I. That children ought to have the consent of their parents in making choice of their calling, and not place themselves as they please, is evident by the approved practise of the Saints recorded in Gods word. #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 28. 2.] #emph[Jacob] was sent by his parents to #emph[Laban] to be educated under him. #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 16. 11, 19.] #emph[David] was appointed by his father to keep sheep: when #emph[Saul] was desirous to have #emph[David] attend upon him, he sent to #emph[Ishai, David's] father, for him. In that #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 17. 17.] #emph[Ishai] was so careful to send provision to his three eldest sons that followed #emph[Saul] to the war, we may well think, that they went to the war with his consent. It is noted of #footnote[#emph[Jer.] 35. 7.] #emph[Jonadab,] that he appointed his sons to dwell in tents, and that accordingly they did so, and are commended and rewarded for this their obedience. It is collected both by #footnote[Justin. Mart. in Dial. cum Tryph. contr. Jud.] ancient and #footnote[#emph[Erasm. paraphr. in Mar.] 6. #emph[Bez annot. in Mar.] 6.] later Divines, that our Lord Jesus Christ #footnote[#emph[Basil. lib. de vit. solit. cap.] 15. #emph[Theod. Eccl. hist. l.] 3. 6. 23.] in his younger years, before he began to exercise his public ministry, occupied himself in his fathers trade: and that this was the thing wherein he manifested his subjection to his parents. This collection is made by comparing #emph[Luk.] 2. 51. (where his subjection is noted) with #emph[Mar.] 6. 3. And #emph[Mat.] 13. 55. Where he is called #footnote[#emph[Muscul. in Mat.] 13. #emph[Danaeus schol. in Mar.] 6. #emph[Gualt. hom.] 46. #emph[in Mar. Muthes. in Mar.] 6. #emph[alijque. Reasons.]] the #emph[Carpenter,] and #emph[the Carpenters son.]

Equity requireth that parents should have an hand in placing both their children, because they brought them forth into the world, and brought them up with much care, pains, and charge, while they were young, and till they were fit for a calling.

Besides, God hath laid it as a charge upon parents, that they should see their children well trained up: great reason therefore #footnote[1\. Equity.] that parents consent be had in setting forth children to a calling. #footnote[2\. Gods charge. #emph[Bellarm. de Mon. lib.] 2. #emph[cap.]]

=== §. 14. #emph[Of the unlawfulness of childrens entering into religious orders without consent of parents.]

Contrary is the opinion of Papists, who say, that children may #footnote[36\. #emph[Licet filijs ingredi religionem inuitis parentibus.]] enter into religious orders, not only without consent, but also against the mind and good like of their parents. Whereby they do not only patronize apparent disobedience in children, against the express word of God; but also disable children from helping their parents in case of necessity: for both which Christ rebuked the Scribes and Pharisees in a like case.

#emph[Object.] Papists do grant that if parents be in such necessity, #footnote[Conditio est, vt parentes non sint in tali necessitate, vt sine filiorum auxilio viuere non possint. Tunc enim tenentur ex praecepto Dei parentibus adesse. Bellarm. loc. cit.] as they cannot live without their childrens help, their children may not by entering into any religious order forsake their parents. For they are bound by the law of God to succour their parents.

#emph[Answ.] 1. This caution hath been extorted from them by evidence of argument taken from Gods word, and pressed by their adversaries.

2. It toucheth not the principal argument taken from Gods precept, which they make of none effect by this their tradition.

3. Though parents be not at that present, when children first enter into their religious order, in such extreme need, yet they may be afterwards. But after that children are once entered, they hold it utterly unlawful that children for any necessity of the parent, should attend upon them for their succour.

#emph[Object.] Children being entered into religious orders may #footnote[Eos iuvant quomodo religiosos decet, nimirum precibus apud Deum. Bellarm. ibid.] help them, as becometh religious persons, by their prayers to God.

#emph[Answ.] 1. This is jump the Pharisees Corban, whereof Christ maketh mention, #emph[Mar.] 7. 11. And whereby he notably discovereth the hypocrisy of the Pharisees, who made pretence of religion, an hindrance to that obedience which God required of children.

2. To pray for that which a man indevoureth not to do, when he may do it, is a plain mocking of God.

The arguments which they allege for confirmation of their erroneous opinion, are taken from extraordinary examples, or from mystical resemblances, as

- 1\. #emph[Abraham's] leaving his fathers house. #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 12. 1. #emph[Deut.] 33. 9.]
- 2\. #emph[Levies] speech of his father and mother, who said, #emph[I have not seen him.]
- 3\. The advice given to the royal Queen, #emph[Forget thy fathers] #footnote[#emph[Psal.] 45. 10.] #emph[house.]
- 4\. The trial of our love of Christ by loving him more #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 10. 37.] then father or mother.
- 5\. Christs forbidding one that followed him to go and #footnote[#emph[Luk.] 9. 60.] bury his father.

#emph[Answ.] 1. For #emph[Abraham's] example, 1. It cannot be proved that he left his fathers house without the consent of his father. 2. He was then married, and so of another house. 3. He had #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 12. 1.] an express particular charge of God to leave his fathers house, even as he had to sacrifice his son. Except the like charge can be showed, his example maketh nothing to the purpose.

2. For #emph[Levies] speech, 1. It was noted by #emph[Moses] in relation #footnote[#emph[Exo.] 32. 26, #emph[etc.]] to a particular zealous fact of the #emph[Levites] in executing the vengeance of the Lord, and so to be reckoned among such extraordinary things as are not exemplary. 2. That which moved the Levites to make no difference betwixt their parents and others, was the Lords cause: their parents and kindred as well as others had notoriously sinned against God, and in that respect the Levites took no notice of them. But they are not such parents which Papists teach children to forsake, but any parents. Now what consequence is this; Some children have been Gods Ministers in executing just punishment on their wicked parents, therefore children may enter into such places as shall exempt them from helping any parents, though well deserving? 3. The Levites had an express charge for that which they did: but that which Papists infer from their #footnote[#emph[Exo.] 32. 27.] example doth make the commandment of God of none effect. #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 15. 6.]

3. For the #footnote[#emph[Psal.] 45. 10.] advice to the Queen, 1. It is mystically to be taken. 2. If it should be literally taken, it is to be taken as given to her after marriage, when she was out of her parents government. 3. It hath relation to the #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 2. 24.] law of marriage, and implieth not a simple forsaking of parents, but a preferring of a husband before them.

4. For the loving of father and mother more then Christ, …. It doth not necessarily imply a forsaking of our parents: for we may love Christ more then them, and yet perform duty to them. 2. If they be forsaken, it must be in opposition to Christ, that either Christ or they must be forsaken, in that if we cleave to them they will draw us from Christ.

5. For Christs forbidding one that followed him to go and bury his father: 1. It was because of an extraordinary calling which he had. 2. It is set down as a pattern to Ministers, to show that they should especially attend upon their proper function, and leave other secular matters to be performed by such as can perform them well enough. To apply it to childrens forsaking of parents, is to pervert the sense of it.

Thus we see to how little purpose the forenamed arguments are alleged to prove that erroneous opinion of childrens entering into religious orders without their parents consent. I might further show how irreligious their pretended religious orders be, and so show how unlawful it is to enter into them, even with consent of parents: but that maketh nothing to the point in hand.

=== §. 15. #emph[Of the unlawfulness of childrens travelling, and binding themselves prentices without consent of parents.]

Contrary also to the forenamed part of childrens obedience, is the practise of such children as travel, and seek their fortunes (as they speak) without consent of parents, like the #emph[Prodigal child,] if not worse: for it is likely that he forced #footnote[#emph[Luk.] 15. 12, #emph[etc.]] from his father a general consent, in that he obtained of him his portion of goods. These usually bring great grief to their parents, and many times make them fear more then is cause, as old #emph[Jacob] feared, when he knew not what was become of #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 37. 35.] his son.

Among those aberrations may be reckoned a custom in this land more usual then lawful, for children to bind themselves prentices without consent of parents: to which fault they who take indentures of such children, or otherwise covenant with them without knowledge of their parents consent, make themselves accessary. #footnote[Arguments to prove that children may not marry without parents consent.]

=== §. 16. #emph[Of parents consent to the marriage of their children.]

II. That children ought to have their parents consent unto their marriage is without all question evident. For

1. God himself hath given us herein a pattern: He first brought the woman to the man #emph[(Gen.] 2. 22.) whereby he would show that he who gave a being to the woman, had a right to dispose her in marriage: which right parents now have: for from them under God, children receive their being. In this case parents stand in Gods room, and are as it were Gods hand to join their children in marriage.

2. God hath given express laws concerning this point. To omit that general moral law, #emph[honour thy father and thy mother] (which, as it is the ground of all other duties appertaining to children, so of this also) the authority and charge which God by his law #emph[(Deut.] 7. 3.) hath laid upon parents, #emph[to give their daughters to husbands, and to take wives for their sons,] hath the force of a law to bind children from taking wives or husbands, without or against their parents consent. This law was not proper to the Jews only; but as a branch of the moral law it is pressed upon Christians, 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 36. 37.

To this may be added the judicial law (if it be to be accounted merely judicial) of a parents power in giving his daughter, or refusing to give her in marriage to him that had deflowered her, #emph[Exo.] 22. 17.

3. Answerable to the law hath been the practise of Gods Saints recorded and approved in Scripture. #emph[Isaac] married the wife which his father provided #emph[(Gen.] 24. 67.) #emph[Jacob] both obeyed his father in going to #emph[Labans] house for a wife #emph[(Gen.] 28. 2.) and also when he came to #emph[Laban] asked his daughter of him #emph[(Gen.] 29. 18. etc.)

Though #emph[Samson] saw a daughter of the Philistines which #footnote[#emph[Non est virginalis pudoris eligere maritum: electionem mariti parentibus deferat. Ambr. de Patr. l.] 1. #emph[c.] 9.] pleased him well, yet would he not marry her before he had his parents consent, #emph[Judg.] 14. 2.

4. These words of #emph[Thamar] (2 #emph[Sam.] 13. 13.) #emph[Speak unto the King] (who was her father,) #emph[for he will not with-hold thee from me,] show that children were not wont to be married without consent of parents: Which is further confirmed by this oath of the Israelites, #emph[There shall not any of us give his daughter] #footnote[Legitimum non est coniugium nisi vxor petatur a parentibus. Euarist. in epist. ad eccles. Affric.] #emph[unto Benjamin to wife, Judg.] 21. 1.

5. #footnote[#emph[Filij sine consensu parentum ritè & iure nubere non possunt. Tertul. ad vxor. l.] 2.] The ancient fathers of the Church have in their ages taught children this duty, and pronounced marriages of children without consent of parents, to be unlawful.

6. The very #footnote[#emph[Tempore creuit amor: tedae quoque iure coissent: Sed vetuere patres. Ouid. Metam.] 4. #emph[Me parenti potius quàm amori obsequi oportet. Teren. in Hecyra.]. #emph[Eurip. in Androm.]] heathen have observed the equity hereof. Though #emph[Shechem] loved #emph[Dinah,] and had deflowered her, yet would he not marry her without the consent of his and her father, #emph[Gen.] 34. 3. etc. #emph[Ismael] had learned as much either by the instruction he had received out of #emph[Abraham's] house, or else by the light of nature; for he stood to the choice which his mother made for him, #emph[Gen.] 21. 21.

7. Though #footnote[#emph[Concil. Trid. decr. de reform. matr. c.] 1.] Papists in other cases make the authority of parents to be of no effect, yet in this case they count it utterly unlawful for children to marry without or against their parents consent: and have thereupon made Canons against it.

8. The #footnote[#emph[Matris voluntatem in tradenda filia omnibus natura praeponit. August. epist.] 233.] law of nature and nations, the civil and canon law, the common and statute law of our Land, all manner of law is agreeable to Gods law in this point.

9. #footnote[#emph[Cum sponsus & sponsa benedicendi sunt a sacerdote, offerantur ecclesiae a parentibus. Grat.] 30. #emph[quaest.] 5. #emph[Cav.]] It hath been a custom in all Christian Churches throughout all ages, for the parent, or some in the parents #footnote[#emph[Quod ipsi Philosophi mirati sunt, seruate virgines. Ambr. de patr. l.] 1. #emph[c.] 9.] room, to give the bride to the bridegroom at the time of the marriage: whereby the parents consent is openly manifested.

10. #footnote[#emph[Erasm. in colloq. de Matrim. Luth. in serm. de Matrim. Bucer. in Mat.] 19. #emph[Calu. Instit. l.] 4. #emph[c.] 19. §. 37. #emph[Bez. de Diuort. Zanch. de Oper. Dei par.] 3. #emph[l.] 4. #emph[c.] 1. #emph[Chemn. in Exam. part.] 2. #emph[Brent. in Catec. Melanc. loc. de coniug. Perkins in Oecon. c.] 6. alijs.] Many Divines of good note and name have judged such marriages as have been made simply without, or directly against parents consent (especially if parents have just cause of exception against those marriages) to be of no force till the parent be brought to ratify them: and in many Churches upon due examination of the matter, they use to account them as no marriages. Experience hath manifested the boldness of many children in setting light by their parents consent in those places where marriages once consummated are ratified, #footnote[#emph[Hoc fieri debere & ciuilis & naturalis ratio suadet. Iustin. Instit. l.] 1. #emph[tit.] 10. #emph[Digest. l.] 23. #emph[tit.] 2] and made indissoluble, though they have been made simply without or directly against parents consent. Many children think, though it be unlawfully done, yet being done it shall stand. Whereupon if they doubt of their parents consent, they will cast how to get their marriage consummate, so as their parents may not know of it to hinder it before it is done: and after it is done, impudently resolve to bear out as well as they can, the storm of their parents displeasure. To prevent such contempt of the power of parents, and to establish that authority which God hath given them over their children, marriages without or against parents consent as aforesaid, are in many Churches made void.

=== §. 17. #emph[Of the equity of the point, and reasons why children should have their parents consent unto their marriage.]

1. By marriage children are put from their parents: for #footnote[The reasons alleged §.] #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 2. 24.] #emph[man must leave his father and mother, and cleave unto his wife.] Is it not then great reason that they from whom children #footnote[12\. may be applied to this particular.] had their being, and by whom they have been maintained and trained up till the time of their marriage, should have notice of that kind of leaving them, and consent thereto?

2. A parents power by the marriage of his child is passed over to the husband or wife of the child. And shall such a power be taken away without consent of parent?

3. Children for the most part being heady and rash for want of experience; and seeking more to satisfy their present carnal desire, then to provide a good lasting help for themselves: but parents by the instinct of nature loving their children as well as children love themselves, and having by much experience better understanding of a meet help, and better able to use their discerning gift in this case, because it is not their own case, and yet the case of one whom they love as themselves, and to whom they wish as much good as to themselves; is it not meet even for the childs good, that in a matter of such moment as marriage, the parent should have a stroke?

=== §. 18. #emph[Of a childs carriage in case a parent provide an unfit mate or none at all.]

#emph[Quest.] What if parents urge their children to marry such #footnote[Whether parents may force their children to marry, or no.] as they can not affect and love: must children therein against their mind and liking yield obedience?

#emph[Answ.] If there be no just exception against the party commended, they ought with the uttermost of their power to endeavour #footnote[See Treat. 6. §. 54.] to bring their affection to the bent of their parents will: and as an help thereunto, be persuaded that their parents are as careful of their good as they themselves are, and wiser then themselves: yea above all they ought to make instant prayer unto God #emph[(in whose hand mans heart is to turn it] #footnote[#emph[Prov.] 21. 1.] #emph[whither soever he will)] that he would be pleased to alter the course of their affection, and to settle it on the party whom their parent hath chosen for them; if at least they see no just cause to the contrary. But if notwithstanding all the means that they can use, they still find their heart altogether averse, they may in a reverend manner entreat their parent to forbear to press that match, and to think of some other.

2. #emph[Quest.] What if the parent be negligent, and in due time provide no fit match, may not the child provide one for himself?

#emph[Answ.] A parents negligence is not a sufficient pretext to make a child cast off that subjection which he oweth to his parent. Yet I deny not but that a child knowing where a fit match is to be had, may make known as much to his parent (as #emph[Samson] did) and crave both his consent and help thereunto. #footnote[#emph[Judg.] 14. 2.] And if his parent give no ear to his humble suit, he may use the mediation of his kindred or other friends. Yea if necessity require that the child be married, and his parent add wilfulness to negligence, and will not be moved at all, neither by the humble suit of his child, nor by the earnest solicitation of any friends, means may be made to the Magistrate (who is in Gods place over the parent as well as over #footnote[#emph[Qui liberos iniuria prohibuerint ducere vxores vel nubere, per praesides cogantur in matrimonium collocare Digest. lib.] 23. #emph[tit.] 2. § 19.] the child, and ought to afford relief unto the child) and what the Magistrate doth in that case is as good a warrant to the child as if the parent had done it.

The like means may be used if a parent be an Idolater, Heretic, or Atheist, and will not yield that his child be married to any but to one of his own profession and disposition.

=== §. 19. #emph[Of the sin of Children in marrying without their parents consent.]

Contrary is the mind and practise of such children as over lightly esteeming their parents power, take matches of their own choice: and that sometimes privily without giving any notice at all to their parents: and sometimes most rebelliously against their parents mind and charge: not much unlike those who in the old world are condemned for #emph[taking wives of all that] #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 6. 2.] #emph[they chose] (which was one branch of that wickedness for which the world was drowned) or rather like #emph[Esau] who took #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 26. 35.] such wives as proved a grief to his parents. What blessing can be expected to fall upon such marriages? Or rather what curse may not be feared to follow them? Gods law is transgressed thereby: his Image in parents despised, that which is more proper to them then any goods; or fraudulently, or violently taken from them: their souls grieved thereat: and they #footnote[See more mischiefs following such marriages, §. 21.] oft provoked to cast off their children, and curse their marriages. Now Gods curse doth oft follow the just curse of a parent.

=== §. 20. #emph[Of objections for childrens marrying without parents consent, answered.]

1. #emph[Object.] Though #emph[Jacob] married one wife according to his parents direction, yet he married other three (at least the two maids) without their consent.

1. #emph[Answ. Jacob's] example in marrying more wives then one is not justifiable.

2. #emph[Answ. Jacob] had a general consent of his parents to #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 28. 2.] take a wife of the daughters of #emph[Laban:] if therefore his marrying of two wives had been lawful, neither this nor that daughter had been taken without all consent of his parents. As for the two maids of whom he had children, neither of them was his wife: for long after they had children they are called #emph[his maids,] #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 32. 22.] and distinguished from his wives.

2. #emph[Object.] Servants may marry without their masters consent: why then not children without their parents?

1. #emph[Answ.] #footnote[See Treat. 8. §. 17.] It is not lawful for servants so to do while the date of their covenant lasteth.

2. #emph[Answ.] Though the servitude of a servant be greater then of a child, yet a parent hath in many respects a greater power over his child, then a master over his servant. The power which a master hath is by a mutual covenant betwixt him and his servant, and by the voluntary subjection of a servant unto his master. But the power of a parent is by the bond of nature, in that a child hath his being from his parents. Besides, this subjection of a child to his parents in case of marriage, is not for servitude but #footnote[See the third reason in §. 17.] for the good of the child.

3. #emph[Object.] Children marry for themselves and not for their parents, why then should parents consent be so much stood upon?

1. #emph[Answ.] Though they marry not #emph[for] their parents, yet they marry #footnote[See the second reason in §. 17. Psal. 127. 3.] #emph[from] their parents: by marriage they are freed from the power of their parents.

2. #emph[Answ.] Children are not their own: they are #emph[the inheritance of the Lord:] the Lord hath given them to parents as an inheritance: a child therefore may no more marry for himself without consent of parents, then alienate his parents goods for himself.

=== §. 21. #emph[Of stealing children from parents for marriage sake.]

To the #footnote[§. 19.] forenamed sin, and to the vengeance thereof, do they make themselves accessary, who fraudulently allure, or violently take away children to marry them otherwise then their parents would. This is a worse kind of felony then stealing away the goods of a man. For children are much more properly a mans own, then his goods: and dearer to him then any goods can be: yea and so much more highly to be esteemed, by how much reasonable creatures are to be preferred before senseless, and sensual things. Our #footnote[#emph[Anno] 4. & 5. #emph[Phil. & Mar. stat.] 8.] statute law expressly condemneth this, and imposeth a severe punishment on such as shall offend therein. And justly do such offenders deserve to be severely punished, both in regard of the heinousness of the sin, and also in regard of the many #footnote[Mischiefs arising from marriages without parents consent.] mischiefs which follow thereon, as, #emph[Alienation of parents affection from their children, Disinheriting heirs, Enmity betwixt the friends of each party so married, Litigious suits in law, Ruin of families,] and (if the personages, whose children are married without their parents consent, be great and noble) #emph[Disturbance of whole towns, cities, and nations.] Instance the destruction of the #emph[Shechemites Gen.] 34. #footnote[#emph[Causa mali tanti coniux. Virg. Aeneid.] 6. #emph[Qui rapta longum cum coniuge bellum Attulit in patriam. Ouid. Met. l.] 12. #emph[lege. Hor. Carm. l.] 1. #emph[Od.] 15.] This is said to have been the cause of the ten years war betwixt the Grecians and Trojans, and of the ruin of Troy.

=== §. 22. #emph[Of ministers sin in marrying children without parents consent.]

Such ministers also as through #emph[Carelessness,] not taking due account of the parties whom they marry, whether they have their parents consent or no; or through #emph[bribery,] being hired by reward, do marry such children as they know have not their parents consent; do in an high degree make themselves accessary to the #footnote[§. 19.] forenamed sin. Their fact is as bad as the fact of the principals themselves. Their solemnization of such marriages emboldneth both the parties that are so married, and also all the persons that are present thereat. They highly dishonour Gods holy ordinance, in that bearing the person of God they say of such as God hath forbidden to be so joined together, #emph[Those whom God hath joined together let no man put asunder.] If ministers had not their hand in such unlawful marriages, they could not be made: for our Church ratifieth no marriage but what is made by a minister. Wherefore some minister or other is guilty of this foul sin, whensoever any child is married without consent of parents. Well therefore doth our Church (to prevent this sin) expressly forbid ministers to marry any without parents consent: and inflict a severe censure on them that shall offend therein.

=== §. 23. #emph[Of childrens forbearing to dispose any of their parents goods without consent.]

A third branch of the subjection of children in forbearing to do any thing without their parents consent, is about their parents #emph[goods.]

That children though living in their parents house, ought not without their parents consent to dispose their goods, is evident by the extent of their obedience, #emph[in all things.]

In that #emph[Isaac] was pleased to send #emph[Jacob] to #emph[Padan Aram] without any great provision, it seemeth that #emph[Jacob] made conscience of taking any thing privily, but went as his father sent him #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 32. 10.] #emph[with his staff.] And the #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 31. 36.] apology which he made to #emph[Laban] his father in law concerning things taken away, showeth that he held it unlawful for children privily to convey away their parents goods. #emph[What is my trespass? What is my sin?] (saith he) #emph[what hast thou found of all my household-stuff?]

Doth he not hereby imply, that if #emph[Labans] daughters had taken away any of their fathers goods, it had been a trespass and sin?

The Apostle saith of the heir (who of all the children may #footnote[#emph[Reasons.] 1. Children under parents are as servants.] seem to have the greatest right) that as #emph[long as he is a child] (that is, under the government of his parents) #emph[he differeth nothing from a servant, though he be Lord of all.] If he differ not from a servant, what right can he have at his pleasure #footnote[#emph[Gal.] 4. 1.] to dispose his parents goods? Hath a servant any such right?

It is very requisite that children herein should be tied to their parents consent, both for the good of parents, and of children themselves.

#emph[Of parents,] that they may know what they have, or have #footnote[2\. Good of parents.] not, and accordingly order their expenses. How can parents tell what they have, if children privily without their knowledge purloin and dispose their goods?

#emph[Of children,] that their lavish humor might by this means #footnote[3\. Good of children.] be restrained: (for youth is much prone beyond moderation to spend, if it have wherewithal) and that their parents may the better #emph[lay up for them.] #footnote[2 #emph[Cor.] 12. 14.]

=== §. 24. #emph[Of the sin of children in purloining and wasting their parents goods.]

Contrary is both the opinion and practise of many #footnote[It is a sin for children.] children.

For #emph[opinion,] many think and say, that whatsoever is their #footnote[1\. To mutter at their parents allowance.] parents, is theirs also: and thereupon being through the watchful eye and provident care of their parents restrained from overlavish spending, or from laying out any thing with their own hands, they murmur against that restraint.

Knowledge and persuasion of their subjection in this case, would be a good means to suppress that repining humour.

For #emph[practise,] #footnote[2\. Privily to purloin their parents goods. #emph[Gen.] 31. 19. Who counsel children to convey away parents goods are accessary to theft.]

1. Some privily take away and purloin what goods, money, wares or any thing else they can come by of their parents. This the holy Ghost accounteth plain theft: for #emph[Rachel] having privily taken away her fathers idols, the Scripture saith that she #emph[stole them.]

Hereunto do they make themselves accessary, who counsel and encourage children so to do: as many busybodies, and deceitful persons advise daughters, when God hath taken away their mothers, to take away linen, and other-like household-stuff from their father, pretending that their father may marry another wife, who will carry all away: And upon like pretence also persuade sons when their fathers die, to convey away what they can from their mother. But such pretences are no sufficient warrant unto children to deceive their parents. It were better for children to be deprived of their parents goods, then to enjoy them with such deceit: for they will be like that #emph[bread which is sweet to a man, and afterward his] #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 20. 17.] #emph[mouth is filled with gravel.]

2. Others riotously spend their portion, like the prodigal #footnote[3\. Wastefully to spend. #emph[Luk.] 15. 13.] child, and run into debt, and so make their parents either to pay it, or to leave them to the law. Many Scholars at the Universities, Gentlemen at Inns of Court, and such children as are somewhat liberally trained up in their parents, or other friends houses, do much offend herein. Little doth this excess and riot differ from the forenamed kind of theft: and ordinarily it bringeth as many mischiefs as that doth.

3. Some also be so ungracious and ungrateful, that being #footnote[4\. To defeat their parents of their goods cunningly.] come to years, and their parents grown old, seek to defeat their parents of all they have, and to bring their parents under them, to be ordered by them: labouring to get possession of all before their parents be dead, or before they be willing to resign any such right unto their children. Such were #footnote[2 #emph[Sam.] 15. 10. #emph[etc.]] #emph[Absalom,] and #emph[Adoniah.] How highly displeasing such practises are to God, the vengeance which fell upon the pates of #footnote[1 #emph[King.] 1. 5. #emph[etc.]] those two #emph[brethren in evil,] traitorous, and disloyal children, doth show.

All such children as seek after the forenamed, or any other like means to defraud their parents, do very ill repay their parents care over them, and more like Barbarians, then Christians, recompense evil for good: they oft bring poverty and ignominy upon their parents and themselves: they are worse then other thieves, because they are more dearly accounted of, and more freely trusted: yea they are a very bad example to servants in the house, or subjects in the commonwealth.

=== §. 25. #emph[Of childrens contentedness to be apparelled after their parents mind and liking.]

IIII. A fourth branch of the foresaid subjection of children is about their apparel, that it be no other, then may stand with their parents good liking. It is noted that #emph[Israel] #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 37. 3.] #emph[made Joseph a coat,] doth not the particular mentioning of that circumstance show, that parents must have the ordering of their childrens apparel? Which is also intimated in the reason given of #emph[Tamars garment of divers colours,] namely because #footnote[2 #emph[Sam.] 13. 18.] #emph[with such garments were the kings daughters, that were virgins, apparelled.] And whereas #emph[Rebekah] had the keeping of her #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 27. 15.] son #emph[Esaus] clothes, it appeareth that his clothes were to the mind of his parents: else he would have hid them from them: for further confirmation whereof it is noted that his apparel was pleasing to his father.

Contrary is the vain-glorious humour of many children, #footnote[Childrens sin in apparelling themselves to the grief, and disgrace of their parents.] who to the grief and discredit of their parents, apparel themselves both against the mind, and also above the ability, and unbeseeming the place and calling of their parents. Among others, many ministers children bring much discredit on their parents hereby. Let all such proud Youths note how the Lord hath threatened to #emph[visit] even #emph[Kings children that are clothed] #footnote[#emph[Zeph.] 1. 8.] #emph[with strange apparel.]

=== §. 26. #emph[Of childrens forbearing to bind themselves to do any thing against their parents consent.]

V. The fifth and last branch wherewith I will exemplify the forenamed subjection of children, shall be that which is expressly noted in the law, namely a childes binding of it self by a vow.

The law giveth the parent power to disannul his childes #footnote[#emph[Num.] 30. 4.] vow. It is therefore a childes duty to abstain from vowing without his parents consent.

Contrary are such vows as Papists allure children to make, #footnote[Unlawful vows of children.] namely, vows of #emph[continency, perpetual virginity, regular obedience, voluntary poverty,] with the like.

Though by these they be not drawn to forsake their parents (which #footnote[§. 14.] before we proved to be utterly unlawful:) and though these in their nature were lawful (which they are not, because they are against Gods law and ordinance, and against Christian liberty, and savour too rankly of Judaism, yea of a worse superstition,) yet without parents consent might they not be made.

As unlawful are oaths, and other like means, whereby children bind themselves to the performance of such indifferent things, as their parents are not willing they should do. What doth this but bring a snare upon the consciences of children, and cause a necessity of breaking one of Gods commandments? Either the third, in breaking their vow or oath; or the fifth, in disobeying their parents.
